1
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:11,630
Can't get the monkey off your back?

2
00:00:12,920 --> 00:00:15,180
Then put it in your mouth...

3
00:00:15,390 --> 00:00:17,260
with Monkeyshine Beer!

4
00:00:20,770 --> 00:00:22,100
Wow.

5
00:00:26,600 --> 00:00:27,980
Monkeyshine Beer.

6
00:00:28,150 --> 00:00:30,480
Because it's a jungle out there!

7
00:00:31,150 --> 00:00:33,280
That commercial always makes me so sad.

8
00:00:33,490 --> 00:00:36,700
Yeah, but then the guy opens his beer and girls run at him...

9
00:00:36,950 --> 00:00:38,910
so everything works out okay.

10
00:00:39,450 --> 00:00:42,330
I meant because the monkey in it reminds me of Marcel.

11
00:00:42,580 --> 00:00:43,700
I can see that.

12
00:00:43,910 --> 00:00:48,500
Because they both have those big brown eyes and the little pouty chin.

13
00:00:48,840 --> 00:00:51,380
And the fact that they're both monkeys?

14
00:00:52,300 --> 00:00:54,970
I wonder if I did the right thing, giving him away.

15
00:00:55,180 --> 00:00:59,470
Ross, you had to. He was humping everything in sight.

16
00:00:59,680 --> 00:01:03,560
I mean, I have a Malibu Barbie who will no longer be wearing white to her wedding.

17
00:01:06,140 --> 00:01:08,400
Remember when he'd borrow your hat...

18
00:01:08,610 --> 00:01:12,360
and when you got it back, there'd be little monkey raisins in it?

19
00:01:13,070 --> 00:01:16,820
When he did it, it was funny. When I did it to my boss's hat...

20
00:01:18,070 --> 00:01:21,450
All of a sudden, I had this big "attitude problem."

21
00:01:26,710 --> 00:01:31,240
∮ So no one told you life was gonna be this way ∮

22
00:01:31,780 --> 00:01:35,950
∮ Your job's a joke, you're broke, your love life's D.O.A. ∮

23
00:01:36,860 --> 00:01:40,670
∮ It's like you're always stuck in second gear ∮

24
00:01:41,130 --> 00:01:43,230
∮ When it hasn't been your day, ∮

25
00:01:43,230 --> 00:01:46,230
∮ your week, your month, or even your year ∮

26
00:01:46,230 --> 00:01:50,190
∮ but I'll be there for you ∮

27
00:01:50,190 --> 00:01:52,020
∮ When the rain starts to pour ∮

28
00:01:52,020 --> 00:01:55,140
∮ I'll be there for you ∮

29
00:01:55,140 --> 00:01:56,980
∮ Like I've been there before ∮

30
00:01:56,980 --> 00:01:59,990
∮ I'll be there for you ∮

31
00:01:59,990 --> 00:02:03,650
∮ 'Cause you're there for me too ∮

32
00:02:10,750 --> 00:02:11,830
Hey. Hey.

33
00:02:12,000 --> 00:02:14,670
- Check it out! Guess what I got?     - Rhythm?

34
00:02:15,710 --> 00:02:17,880
No. My first fan mail!

35
00:02:18,050 --> 00:02:19,470
Check it out.

36
00:02:19,760 --> 00:02:21,470
"Dear Dr. Ramoray:

37
00:02:21,640 --> 00:02:24,810
Know that I love you and would do anything to have you."

38
00:02:24,970 --> 00:02:28,020
My gosh! "Your not-so-secret admirer, Erika Ford."

39
00:02:29,560 --> 00:02:30,730
Oh, wait.

40
00:02:30,900 --> 00:02:35,400
"P.S. Enclosed, please find 14 of my eyelashes."

41
00:02:36,780 --> 00:02:39,990
You know, in Crazy World, that means you're married.

42
00:02:42,450 --> 00:02:45,870
It's not addressed to Days Of Our Lives. This came to your apartment.

43
00:02:46,040 --> 00:02:48,660
There's no stamp on it. This woman was in our building.

44
00:02:48,830 --> 00:02:50,250
Oh, my God.

45
00:02:51,000 --> 00:02:53,000
I got my very own stalker!

46
00:02:55,460 --> 00:02:56,670
- Hey, guys.     - Hey.

47
00:02:56,840 --> 00:02:58,170
- Hey.     - Ooh.

48
00:02:58,340 --> 00:02:59,420
Where are you off to?

49
00:02:59,590 --> 00:03:02,300
There's this paleontology conference in L.A.

50
00:03:02,510 --> 00:03:06,560
So I figured I'd go and drive down to the zoo and surprise Marcel.

51
00:03:06,760 --> 00:03:10,100
I think he will be surprised. Till he realizes he's a monkey...

52
00:03:11,810 --> 00:03:14,480
and, uh, you know, isn't capable of that emotion.

53
00:03:16,110 --> 00:03:19,280
Oh, Phoebe? That really cute guy is here again.

54
00:03:19,490 --> 00:03:20,570
Oh? Oh.

55
00:03:20,740 --> 00:03:21,900
Okay, so everyone, ahem...

56
00:03:22,110 --> 00:03:25,450
pretend I'm telling you a story and it's really funny.

57
00:03:25,660 --> 00:03:27,030
So everyone just laugh. Now!

58
00:03:29,660 --> 00:03:31,500
I know, I know! Oh.

59
00:03:31,660 --> 00:03:32,920
- Hello.     - Hi.

60
00:03:33,080 --> 00:03:34,460
Oh.

61
00:03:34,670 --> 00:03:35,830
I'm Rob Donan.

62
00:03:36,000 --> 00:03:37,130
Hi, Rob Donan.

63
00:03:37,300 --> 00:03:41,590
I don't know anything about music, but I think you're really great.

64
00:03:42,050 --> 00:03:44,430
Oh, wow. Ha, ha.

65
00:03:44,590 --> 00:03:48,390
Anyway, I schedule performers for children's libraries.

66
00:03:48,560 --> 00:03:53,310
And I was thinking, have you thought about playing your songs for kids?

67
00:03:53,480 --> 00:03:55,100
Oh, I would love to have kids...

68
00:03:58,480 --> 00:04:02,360
hear me play the songs that I will write for them.

69
00:04:05,070 --> 00:04:06,620
What do you wanna do for dinner?

70
00:04:06,820 --> 00:04:08,830
We could stay in and cook for ourselves.

71
00:04:14,750 --> 00:04:16,920
- Hello.     - It's Erika.

72
00:04:17,090 --> 00:04:18,840
Oh, my God. The stalker!

73
00:04:19,000 --> 00:04:20,250
Never mind. It's open.

74
00:04:20,420 --> 00:04:22,300
Aah! Aah!

75
00:04:22,720 --> 00:04:25,340
Yes, hitting her with a frying pan is a good idea.

76
00:04:25,510 --> 00:04:29,350
We might wanna have a backup plan, though, just in case she isn't a cartoon.

77
00:04:30,520 --> 00:04:32,180
Let's get out of here!

78
00:04:35,270 --> 00:04:36,850
The one time they're not home!

79
00:04:37,020 --> 00:04:38,690
Okay. Okay.

80
00:04:39,150 --> 00:04:40,770
We'll leave. When we pass her...

81
00:04:40,940 --> 00:04:43,690
she won't know me, because we never met!

82
00:04:45,530 --> 00:04:47,700
That's how radio stars escape stalkers!

83
00:04:49,700 --> 00:04:51,200
She's coming!

84
00:04:57,130 --> 00:04:58,290
- It's me.     - Aah!

85
00:04:58,460 --> 00:05:02,090
Uh, this is it. This is how we're gonna die.

86
00:05:03,670 --> 00:05:06,050
- You ready?     - Wait, wait, wait!

87
00:05:13,100 --> 00:05:14,560
Hi.

88
00:05:14,730 --> 00:05:16,690
Erika!

89
00:05:25,990 --> 00:05:27,530
- Mr. Geller?     - Yes, hi.

90
00:05:27,780 --> 00:05:30,490
Hi. Dean Lipson, zoo administrator.

91
00:05:30,660 --> 00:05:32,450
I was told you had a question.

92
00:05:32,620 --> 00:05:37,330
I, uh... I can't find the monkey I donated last year. He's a capuchin...

93
00:05:37,500 --> 00:05:39,120
answers to the name Marcel?

94
00:05:39,330 --> 00:05:42,920
Ah. I'm afraid I have some bad news.

95
00:05:43,170 --> 00:05:45,590
Marcel has passed on.

96
00:05:47,300 --> 00:05:49,930
Oh, my God. What happened?

97
00:05:50,090 --> 00:05:52,720
Well, he got sick, and then he got sicker...

98
00:05:52,970 --> 00:05:54,970
and then he got a little better.

99
00:05:55,520 --> 00:05:57,140
But then he died.

100
00:05:59,650 --> 00:06:01,100
I can't believe this!

101
00:06:01,310 --> 00:06:06,110
I'm sorry, Mr. Geller. But you know, there's an old saying:

102
00:06:06,280 --> 00:06:08,950
"Sometimes monkeys die."

103
00:06:10,530 --> 00:06:12,950
It's not a great saying...

104
00:06:14,040 --> 00:06:16,330
but it certainly is fitting today.

105
00:06:16,540 --> 00:06:19,460
- Someone should've called me.     - I'm sorry.

106
00:06:19,620 --> 00:06:22,080
Look, I know this can't bring him back...

107
00:06:22,250 --> 00:06:24,710
but here, it's just a gesture.

108
00:06:26,590 --> 00:06:27,840
Zoo dollars?

109
00:06:28,010 --> 00:06:32,340
Yes, and come see the bird show at 4. The macaws wear hats.

110
00:06:33,850 --> 00:06:37,020
Well, it's a lot cuter if your monkey hasn't just died.

111
00:06:42,810 --> 00:06:44,060
Ooh.

112
00:06:44,230 --> 00:06:45,770
I know.

113
00:06:47,320 --> 00:06:49,950
I can't believe Joey's having lunch with his stalker.

114
00:06:50,110 --> 00:06:51,700
What is she like?

115
00:06:51,860 --> 00:06:54,370
- Well, you remember Kathy Bates in Misery?     - Mm-hm.

116
00:06:54,530 --> 00:06:57,700
Well, she looks the exact opposite of that.

117
00:06:58,790 --> 00:06:59,910
And she's not crazy?

118
00:07:00,080 --> 00:07:02,710
Oh, no, no. She's a total wack job.

119
00:07:02,880 --> 00:07:07,670
Yeah. She thinks that Joey is actually Dr. Drake Ramoray.

120
00:07:07,840 --> 00:07:09,260
Oh, my God!

121
00:07:09,420 --> 00:07:11,550
Oh. And he's going out with her?

122
00:07:11,760 --> 00:07:13,430
He cannot pursue this.

123
00:07:13,590 --> 00:07:17,260
Just because this woman thinks she can see Joey through the magical box...

124
00:07:17,430 --> 00:07:20,020
in her living room doesn't mean she's not a person.

125
00:07:20,180 --> 00:07:23,650
Does she not deserve happiness? Does she not deserve love?

126
00:07:24,690 --> 00:07:28,940
What are you looking at me for? He's the one who wants to boff the maniac.

127
00:07:30,030 --> 00:07:31,110
Are you okay?

128
00:07:31,280 --> 00:07:33,450
No, uh-uh. I'm just nervous.

129
00:07:33,610 --> 00:07:36,950
Maybe if I just picture them all in their underwear...

130
00:07:37,120 --> 00:07:40,080
That's not a good idea. That's the reason the last guy got fired.

131
00:07:40,250 --> 00:07:41,710
Oh.

132
00:07:44,040 --> 00:07:45,130
Yeah.

133
00:07:45,290 --> 00:07:47,590
I'm used to playing for grownups.

134
00:07:47,750 --> 00:07:51,300
Grownups drink their coffee and do their grownup thing, you know.

135
00:07:51,470 --> 00:07:52,590
And kids...

136
00:07:52,760 --> 00:07:54,050
listen.

137
00:07:55,470 --> 00:07:58,050
It's just a huge responsibility.

138
00:07:58,220 --> 00:07:59,390
What?

139
00:07:59,560 --> 00:08:01,480
Are you gonna kiss me?

140
00:08:02,770 --> 00:08:03,940
I was thinking about it.

141
00:08:04,650 --> 00:08:06,480
Okay.

142
00:08:08,940 --> 00:08:11,610
Yeah, okay. Alrighty. Let's play some tunes!

143
00:08:14,660 --> 00:08:15,950
Hi, everybody. I'm Phoebe.

144
00:08:16,160 --> 00:08:18,490
Hi, Phoebe!

145
00:08:18,700 --> 00:08:20,990
Ha, ha. Okay. Um...

146
00:08:21,290 --> 00:08:25,210
I'm gonna play, um, some songs about grandparents, okay?

147
00:08:27,590 --> 00:08:30,920
Now Grandma's a person Who everyone likes

148
00:08:31,090 --> 00:08:34,170
She bought you a train And a bright shiny bike

149
00:08:34,340 --> 00:08:37,430
But lately she hasn't Been coming to dinner

150
00:08:37,600 --> 00:08:40,930
And last time you saw her She looked so much thinner

151
00:08:41,970 --> 00:08:45,480
Now your mom and your dad said She moved to Peru

152
00:08:45,650 --> 00:08:48,980
But the truth is she died And someday you will too

153
00:08:49,150 --> 00:08:58,160
La-la-la-la, la-la-la-la La-la-la-la, la-la-la-la-la-la

154
00:08:59,990 --> 00:09:02,910
- Oh, Drake, isn't it amazing?     - Yeah, it is.

155
00:09:04,870 --> 00:09:06,250
What?

156
00:09:07,000 --> 00:09:09,710
Well, here we sit, devil-may-care...

157
00:09:09,880 --> 00:09:14,300
and just a little while ago you were reattaching Simone's spinal cord.

158
00:09:14,470 --> 00:09:15,800
Yeah, that was a tricky one.

159
00:09:17,550 --> 00:09:18,720
In reality...

160
00:09:18,890 --> 00:09:23,060
that operation takes over 10 hours, but they only showed it for two minutes.

161
00:09:23,770 --> 00:09:24,850
Who's "they"?

162
00:09:27,310 --> 00:09:28,400
No one.

163
00:09:30,270 --> 00:09:34,650
Oh, Drake, you are so talented. Let me see those hands.

164
00:09:34,820 --> 00:09:37,280
- Oh, these hands, these beautiful hands.     - Ha, ha.

165
00:09:37,490 --> 00:09:40,240
Ha, ha. Oh, I could just eat them.

166
00:09:40,410 --> 00:09:41,740
- But I won't.     - Ha, ha.

167
00:09:42,240 --> 00:09:44,120
Good. Otherwise my watch would fall off.

168
00:09:50,790 --> 00:09:52,000
No, seriously.

169
00:09:53,590 --> 00:09:54,670
These hands...

170
00:09:54,840 --> 00:09:57,420
these miracle, magical, life-giving hands.

171
00:09:57,680 --> 00:10:01,720
Oh, just to be near them, touch them, maybe even lick one?

172
00:10:02,890 --> 00:10:05,270
- All right. Ha, ha. Just one.     - Ha-ha-ha.

173
00:10:13,070 --> 00:10:14,150
Wow.

174
00:10:14,650 --> 00:10:15,730
You're good at that.

175
00:10:18,360 --> 00:10:20,320
Oh, my God! Someone! He's choking!

176
00:10:20,490 --> 00:10:22,570
Is anyone here a doctor?

177
00:10:22,740 --> 00:10:23,830
Oh, yes.

178
00:10:23,990 --> 00:10:25,830
Yes, the best doctor in Salem!

179
00:10:26,000 --> 00:10:28,120
Dr. Drake Ramoray!

180
00:10:43,260 --> 00:10:47,600
Meet me in the nocturnal house in 15 minutes.

181
00:10:49,560 --> 00:10:54,900
Uh, hey, look. I don't really enjoy being with other men that way.

182
00:10:55,820 --> 00:10:57,940
But, um, zoo dollars?

183
00:10:59,110 --> 00:11:01,570
It's about your monkey.

184
00:11:01,910 --> 00:11:03,740
It's alive.

185
00:11:08,330 --> 00:11:11,120
I don't understand. Why didn't you help that man?

186
00:11:11,290 --> 00:11:14,630
Uh, because, uh, I'm a neurosurgeon...

187
00:11:14,790 --> 00:11:18,710
and that was clearly a case of, uh, uh...

188
00:11:18,880 --> 00:11:20,090
"foodal chokage."

189
00:11:21,760 --> 00:11:22,840
You know.

190
00:11:25,050 --> 00:11:28,060
- I gotta tell you something...     - No, no, no.

191
00:11:28,220 --> 00:11:30,100
You don't have to tell me anything.

192
00:11:30,270 --> 00:11:32,640
You don't have to explain yourself to me. Oh!

193
00:11:32,850 --> 00:11:35,940
Who am I to question the great Dr. Drake Ramoray?

194
00:11:36,110 --> 00:11:38,190
- That's...     - I should be happy to be near you.

195
00:11:38,360 --> 00:11:39,480
Hey, I...

196
00:11:43,360 --> 00:11:44,910
"Hey" what?

197
00:11:45,070 --> 00:11:47,490
That's it. Just hey!

198
00:11:48,200 --> 00:11:50,620
Like at the end of a dance. Hey!

199
00:11:53,290 --> 00:11:55,250
Hey. Ha-ha-ha.

200
00:11:55,420 --> 00:11:57,340
Hey.

201
00:11:58,340 --> 00:12:00,210
There'll be times when you get older

202
00:12:00,380 --> 00:12:02,050
When you'll want to sleep with people

203
00:12:02,220 --> 00:12:05,510
Just to make them like you But don't

204
00:12:06,760 --> 00:12:07,850
'Cause

205
00:12:08,010 --> 00:12:11,470
That's another thing that you don't wanna do

206
00:12:11,640 --> 00:12:12,640
Everybody!

207
00:12:12,810 --> 00:12:17,690
That's another thing that you don't wanna do

208
00:12:19,110 --> 00:12:21,030
- Excellent!     - Very informative!

209
00:12:21,190 --> 00:12:24,030
Not at all inappropriate!

210
00:12:24,200 --> 00:12:28,160
Thank you for coming, everybody. There are cookies in the back.

211
00:12:29,580 --> 00:12:31,080
That was great.

212
00:12:31,250 --> 00:12:32,500
The kids loved you.

213
00:12:33,910 --> 00:12:36,250
Yay! I rock!

214
00:12:36,420 --> 00:12:39,340
And you know why? Because you told the truth.

215
00:12:39,500 --> 00:12:41,670
Nobody ever tells kids the truth.

216
00:12:42,090 --> 00:12:43,550
You were incredible!

217
00:12:43,970 --> 00:12:45,880
Ha, ha. But?

218
00:12:46,430 --> 00:12:49,800
- How did you know there was a "but"?     - I sense these things.

219
00:12:49,970 --> 00:12:52,140
It was either "but" or "butter."

220
00:12:54,020 --> 00:12:56,350
The thing is, I think some of the parents...

221
00:12:56,520 --> 00:12:58,230
they were hoping that...

222
00:12:58,400 --> 00:13:01,360
you'd play more songs about barnyard animals.

223
00:13:01,530 --> 00:13:02,820
I can do that.

224
00:13:02,990 --> 00:13:04,110
- Really?     - Yeah.

225
00:13:04,280 --> 00:13:07,570
- Because that would be fantastic.     - Oh.

226
00:13:08,200 --> 00:13:10,700
What? You wanna kiss me?

227
00:13:10,870 --> 00:13:12,580
I'm thinking about it.

228
00:13:14,910 --> 00:13:16,580
Ah, the bat.

229
00:13:17,170 --> 00:13:19,290
Ambassador of darkness.

230
00:13:19,460 --> 00:13:23,590
Flitting out of his cave like a winged messenger.

231
00:13:23,760 --> 00:13:27,260
Sightless specter of the macabre.

232
00:13:28,430 --> 00:13:29,760
Buddy, my monkey?

233
00:13:30,510 --> 00:13:31,930
Oh, yeah, right.

234
00:13:33,640 --> 00:13:34,890
There was a break-in...

235
00:13:35,060 --> 00:13:37,850
a few months back. Inside job.

236
00:13:38,020 --> 00:13:39,350
Your monkey was taken.

237
00:13:39,560 --> 00:13:41,940
Oh, my God. The zoo told me that my monkey was dead.

238
00:13:42,150 --> 00:13:44,110
Pfft. The zoo!

239
00:13:44,280 --> 00:13:46,820
You believe everything the zoo tells you?

240
00:13:49,280 --> 00:13:52,830
Uh, that's the only thing the zoo's ever told me.

241
00:13:52,990 --> 00:13:56,370
Of course they'll say he's dead. They don't want the bad publicity.

242
00:13:56,540 --> 00:13:58,000
It's all a great big cover-up.

243
00:13:58,170 --> 00:14:02,090
Do you have any idea how high up this thing goes?

244
00:14:02,290 --> 00:14:04,000
That guy Lipson?

245
00:14:04,460 --> 00:14:06,090
Lipson knows?

246
00:14:06,760 --> 00:14:07,840
Hmm.

247
00:14:08,010 --> 00:14:09,630
Lipson knows, huh?

248
00:14:09,800 --> 00:14:12,050
Ah, hello, Mr. Possum.

249
00:14:12,260 --> 00:14:16,020
Enigma of the trees, upside-down denizen of the night.

250
00:14:16,180 --> 00:14:19,310
- Taunting gravity...     - Buddy, my monkey. My monkey?

251
00:14:21,110 --> 00:14:23,020
Word on the street...

252
00:14:23,190 --> 00:14:27,530
Well, when I say "street," I mean those little pretend streets here at the zoo.

253
00:14:27,700 --> 00:14:29,110
Of course.

254
00:14:30,910 --> 00:14:33,450
Your monkey found a new career...

255
00:14:33,620 --> 00:14:37,040
in the entertainment field. That's all I know.

256
00:14:38,460 --> 00:14:39,910
This is unbelievable!

257
00:14:40,080 --> 00:14:45,040
So, what is this information worth to you, my friend?

258
00:14:45,750 --> 00:14:48,590
Are you trying to get me to bribe you?

259
00:14:48,760 --> 00:14:50,300
Maybe.

